1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,040
Imagine you happen across a short movie script that describes a scene between a person and

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:07,540
their AI assistant.

3
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:13,600
The script has what the person asks the AI, but the AI's response has been torn off.

4
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:18,280
Suppose you also have this powerful magical machine that can take any text and provide

5
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:21,440
a sensible prediction of what word comes next.

6
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:25,320
You could then finish the script by feeding in what you have to the machine, seeing what

7
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:30,640
it would predict to start the AI's answer, and then repeating this over and over with

8
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:33,480
a growing script completing the dialogue.

9
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:37,000
When you interact with a chatbot, this is exactly what's happening.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,600
A large language model is a sophisticated mathematical function that predicts what word

11
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:44,440
comes next for any piece of text.

12
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:49,240
Instead of predicting one word with certainty, though, what it does is assign a probability

13
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:51,640
to all possible next words.

14
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:56,160
To build a chatbot, what you do is lay out some text that describes an interaction between

15
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:59,280
a user and a hypothetical AI assistant.

16
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:04,080
You add on whatever the user types in as the first part of that interaction, and then you

17
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:08,960
have the model repeatedly predict the next word that such a hypothetical AI assistant

18
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:13,120
would say in response, and that's what's presented to the user.

19
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:16,880
In doing this, the output tends to look a lot more natural if you allow it to select

20
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:20,120
less likely words along the way at random.

21
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:24,880
So what this means is, even though the model itself is deterministic, a given prompt typically

22
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:28,200
gives a different answer each time it's run.

23
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:33,080
Models learn how to make these predictions by processing an enormous amount of text typically

24
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:34,560
pulled from the internet.

25
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:39,640
For a standard human to read the amount of text that was used to train GPT-3, for example,

26
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:44,880
if they read nonstop 24-7, it would take over 2600 years.

27
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:48,240
Larger models since then train on much, much more.

28
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:52,240
You can think of training a little bit like tuning the dials on a big machine.

29
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:57,160
The way that a language model behaves is entirely determined by these many different continuous

30
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:01,520
values, usually called parameters or weights.

31
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:05,480
Changing those parameters will change the probabilities that the model gives for the

32
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:07,880
next word on a given input.

33
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:12,760
What puts the large in large language model is how they can have hundreds of billions

34
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:15,320
of these parameters.

35
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:18,200
No human ever deliberately sets those parameters.

36
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:22,760
Instead, they begin at random, meaning the model just outputs gibberish, but they are

37
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:27,320
repeatedly refined based on many example pieces of text.

38
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:31,840
One of these training examples could be just a handful of words, or it could be thousands,

39
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:36,120
but in either case, the way this works is to pass in all but the last word from that

40
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:41,640
example into the model and compare the prediction that it makes with the true last word from

41
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:43,240
the example.

42
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:48,480
An algorithm called backpropagation is used to tweak all of the parameters in such a way

43
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:53,160
that it makes the model a little more likely to choose the true last word and a little

44
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,200
less likely to choose all the others.

45
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,960
When you do this for many, many trillions of examples, not only does the model start

46
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:05,560
to give more accurate predictions on the training data, but it also starts to make more reasonable

47
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:09,520
predictions on text that it's never seen before.

48
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:14,320
Given the huge number of parameters and the enormous amount of training data, the scale

49
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:19,600
of computation involved in training a large language model is mind-boggling.

50
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:24,540
To illustrate, imagine that you could perform one billion additions and multiplications every

51
00:03:24,540 --> 00:03:26,000
single second.

52
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,560
How long do you think that it would take for you to do all of the operations involved

53
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:33,400
in training the largest language models?

54
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:36,120
Do you think it would take a year?

55
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:39,000
Maybe something like 10,000 years?

56
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,200
The answer is actually much more than that.

57
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,680
It's well over 100 million years.

58
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,580
This is only part of the story, though.

59
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:49,700
This whole process is called pre-training.

60
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:54,040
The goal of auto-completing a random passage of text from the internet is very different

61
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:56,760
from the goal of being a good AI assistant.

62
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:01,360
To address this, chatbots undergo another type of training, just as important, called

63
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:04,120
reinforcement learning with human feedback.

64
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:10,440
Workers flag unhelpful or problematic predictions, and their corrections further change the model's

65
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:15,600
parameters, making them more likely to give predictions that users prefer.

66
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:20,000
Looking back at the pre-training, though, this staggering amount of computation is only

67
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,600
made possible by using special computer chips that are optimized for running many, many

68
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:27,920
operations in parallel, known as GPUs.

69
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:32,080
However, not all language models can be easily parallelized.

70
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:37,840
Prior to 2017, most language models would process text one word at a time, but then

71
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:43,880
a team of researchers at Google introduced a new model known as the Transformer.

72
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:46,640
Transformers don't read text from the start to the finish.

73
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,840
They soak it all in at once, in parallel.

74
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:54,520
The very first step inside a Transformer, and most other language models for that matter,

75
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:57,840
is to associate each word with a long list of numbers.

76
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:02,520
The reason for this is that the training process only works with continuous values,

77
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:07,120
so you have to somehow encode language using numbers, and each of these list of numbers

78
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:11,000
may somehow encode the meaning of the corresponding word.

79
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,960
What makes Transformers unique is their reliance on a special operation known as attention.

80
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:21,360
This operation gives all of these lists of numbers a chance to talk to one another and

81
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:27,360
refine the meanings that they encode based on the context around, all done in parallel.

82
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:32,000
For example, the numbers encoding the word bank might be changed based on the context

83
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,920
surrounding it to somehow encode the more specific notion of a river bank.

84
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:42,360
Transformers typically also include a second type of operation known as a feed-forward

85
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:47,320
neural network, and this gives the model extra capacity to store more patterns about language

86
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:49,320
learned during training.

87
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:53,880
All of this data repeatedly flows through many different iterations of these two fundamental

88
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:59,880
operations, and as it does so, the hope is that each list of numbers is enriched to encode

89
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:05,280
whatever information might be needed to make an accurate prediction of what word follows

90
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:06,960
in the passage.

91
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:12,000
At the end, one final function is performed on the last vector in this sequence, which

92
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,840
now has had a chance to be influenced by all the other context from the input text, as

93
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:21,640
well as everything the model learned during training, to produce a prediction of the next

94
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:22,640
word.

95
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:28,720
Then the model's prediction looks like a probability for every possible next word.

96
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:33,120
Although researchers design the framework for how each of these steps work, it's important

97
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:38,120
to understand that the specific behavior is an emergent phenomenon based on how those

98
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:42,560
hundreds of billions of parameters are tuned during training.

99
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:47,160
This makes it incredibly challenging to determine why the model makes the exact predictions

100
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:48,480
that it does.

101
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:52,880
What you can see is that when you use large language model predictions to autocomplete

102
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:59,880
a prompt, the words that it generates are uncannily fluent, fascinating, and even useful.

103
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,240
If you're a new viewer and you're curious about more details on how transformers and

104
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:12,520
attention work, boy do I have some material for you.

105
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:17,280
One option is to jump into a series I made about deep learning, where we visualize and

106
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:22,200
motivate the details of attention and all the other steps in a transformer.

107
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:26,040
But also, on my second channel I just posted a talk that I gave a couple months ago about

108
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:29,040
this topic for the company TNG in Munich.

109
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:33,040
Sometimes I actually prefer the content that I make as a casual talk rather than a produced

110
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:37,040
video, but I leave it up to you which one of these feels like the better follow on.

111
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:54,280
Thank you.

